## Workflow
- Every financial POST/PUT/PATCH requires Idempotency-Key header


## Preventing Broken Imports

- Before starting ANY new work, read `docs/CODEBASE-REFERENCE.md` first.
- Every import used in the codebase must be listed in that document. If an import is not listed, it does not exist — do not use it.
- This file is the single source of truth for import paths, package names, service functions, repository functions, and component prop types.
- Always copy the closest working page as a template for new pages and components.
- After any edit, run `npm run build` to catch import errors before committing.

---

## Workflow
- No users.wallet_balance column
- Personal wallet and business earnings in separate tables
- Never trust client values for price, fees, discount, balance, or payment status

## Quality rules
- Every feature handles: loading, empty, error, success, disabled states
- Every list paginates
- Every API returns { ok: true, data } or { ok: false, error }

## Workflow
- Work in one phase at a time. Never skip ahead.
- After each phase: run npm run build, commit, push.
- Ask before proceeding if anything is unclear. Never guess.

## Auth Backend (Phase 3)
- All auth routes run on the Node.js runtime.
- OTP codes hashed with SHA-256. Never stored plain, never logged in
  production.
- Passwords hashed with bcryptjs (rounds=12). Never logged.
- Sessions use crypto.randomBytes(32) tokens; only SHA-256 hash stored.
- Session cookies: HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Secure in production.
- Cookie name: nolida_session.
- Every auth action writes a row to security_events.
- Rate limiting is in-memory for now — must move to Redis before launch.
- Auth service owns all logic; repositories own only SQL.
- Never reveal whether an email/phone exists in password-reset flows.

## Auth UI (Phase 4) — locked rules
- `/` is the sign-in screen. `/login` only ever redirects to `/`. Never restore a landing page at `/`.
- The 6-digit reset code is entered ONLY on `/reset-password`, which consumes it together with the new password. Never add a code step to `/verify` — `otp_records` are single-use and the user would be left with a spent code.
- No OTP resend endpoint exists. RESET codes are re-issued through `/api/auth/forgot-password`; a missing REGISTER code means restarting signup. Do not invent a resend silently.
- Auth forms call `apiFetch` from `@/lib/client/api` and render `error.message`; never surface a raw response or a caught exception.
- Client zod rules are a courtesy. Never relax a server rule to match a form.
- A query-string `identifier` is prefill only. It must never stand in for the OTP check.

## Home Page — Mobile Welcome (Phase 4C)
- On mobile (< 768px), `/` shows a Noise-inspired welcome screen: dark
  navy canvas, large centered logo, short headline, one primary action,
  one secondary sign-in link.
- NO feature lists, NO icon grids, NO hero paragraphs on mobile.
- Desktop (>= 768px) always shows the split layout — unchanged.
- View switch is client state (useState), not a route change.
- CSS media queries control which view renders — never JavaScript.
- The SlimTopBar hides while the welcome canvas is active so the navy runs
  edge to edge; it returns on the login view.
- `/home` is real since Phase 5A: sign-in and verify land on the app shell. Post-auth navigation is a full document load on purpose — the shell must render on the server with the new cookie — so every post-auth `window.location` carries an explicit `no-location-assign-relative-destination` exemption with its reason.
- Money vocabulary is locked by `docs/PRODUCT.md`: plan, subscription, wallet, earnings, virtual card, fintail, eligibility.

## Explore Page (Phase 4D)
- /explore is a single scrollable marketing page.
- It lives in (marketing) so it uses SiteHeader + SiteFooter.
- It reuses existing marketing section components. Do not create new
  section components for it.
- Reachable from: mobile welcome screen (top-right "Explore NOlida →"),
  desktop SlimTopBar, desktop SiteHeader.

## Mobile Welcome Collage (Phase 4D)
- The mobile welcome screen has a blurred collage of the four login
  images behind the content.
- Images are heavily blurred (40px) at low opacity (0.18) so they
  read as ambient texture.
- A dark gradient overlay sits between the collage and the content.
- Animation: 24s slow drift. Respects prefers-reduced-motion.
- The "Explore NOlida →" link sits top-right.

## App Shell (Phase 5A)
- Every signed-in screen lives in `src/app/(main)/`. The session guard is the `redirect()` in `(main)/layout.tsx`; never move it into individual pages, and never render a signed-in screen outside the group.
- `/` sends a visitor who already holds a live session to `/home`.
- Navigation destinations are declared once in `src/components/layout/navigation.ts`. Never hardcode a second copy of a bar's links.
- Screens that are not built yet render `PagePlaceholder` and state their scope. Never fake data on a placeholder — especially balances, counts and badges. Badges render only above zero.
- Sign out goes only through `POST /api/auth/logout`; the client neither reads nor writes the cookie.
- Server Components read the session cookie via `readSessionToken()` (`src/lib/server/auth/session-cookie.ts`); Route Handlers keep using `readSessionCookie(request)`.
- The theme switch in the profile drawer stays disabled until a theme preference can actually be saved.

## Feed and Posts (Phase 5B)
- Post types: GENERAL_POST, BUSINESS_POST, SERVICE_POST, PRODUCT_POST, REQUEST_POST, OFFER_POST, AVAILABILITY_POST, JOB_REQUEST_POST, ANNOUNCEMENT_POST, RECOMMENDATION_POST.
- Post body: 1-5000 characters. Comment body: 1-2000 characters. Enforced by schema, service and DB CHECK constraint — in that order.
- Posts are visible: PUBLIC (anyone signed in), FOLLOWERS (only followers), PRIVATE (only the author). Enforced in SQL by the `VISIBLE_TO_VIEWER` predicate; never filter a page after loading it.
- A post the viewer may not see is 404, never 403. Never leak that a private post exists.
- Feed ordering: `created_at DESC, id DESC`. Strictly chronological. Ranking, when it comes, replaces one repository call — not the caller.
- Pagination is a `(created_at, id)` cursor. Repositories fetch `limit + 1`; the extra row is what makes `nextCursor` honest.
- `liked`/`saved` are attached per-viewer with two batched `= ANY($1)` lookups per page. Never one query per post.
- All interactions are idempotent. Liking twice is a no-op. Enforce it with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` plus a UNIQUE constraint — never a read-then-write.
- A counter and its row move inside one `withTransaction`. Counters are clamped with `GREATEST(0, n + delta)`.
- Optimistic UI for likes and saves only, reverted exactly on failure. Comments and deletes wait for the server.
- Media uploads (photos/videos) are NOT enabled yet — deferred until Cloudinary is configured. The composer shows disabled buttons rather than hiding them or faking an upload.
- `/home` and `/create` share one `PostComposer`. `/favorites` renders the saved feed. `/post/[id]` is signed-in only — a genuinely public post page is a separate decision.
- Feed types and constants live in `src/lib/feed/` (`constants.ts`, `types.ts`), which must stay importable from Client Components.

## Search and Discovery (Phase 6)
- Search is Postgres full-text + trigram for now. `search.repo.ts` is the only file that knows how; a Meilisearch/Typesense swap replaces it and `search.service.ts` without touching a component or a route.
- FTS configuration is `simple`, deliberately: no stemmer, no English stopword list, because those break Nigerian names and languages. Do not "improve" it to `english`.
- Fuzzy post matching uses `<%` (WORD similarity), never `%`. `%` scores two strings whole, so one typo inside a long body scores ~0.18 and never matches.
- **Operand order is load-bearing: needle first — `$2 <% p.body`.** `p.body <% $2` scores the whole post against a short word and matches nothing, with no error to show for it.
- `pg_trgm.word_similarity_threshold` stays at the 0.6 default. Do not lower it in a migration — it is database-wide and every future `<%` query inherits it. Measured trade-offs are in `docs/SEARCH.md`.
- User text is never concatenated into SQL. It goes through `websearch_to_tsquery('simple', $n)`.
- Search reuses `VISIBLE_TO_VIEWER` from `repositories/visibility.ts`, the same predicate `posts.repo.ts` uses. Never filter visibility after loading a page, and never let `counts` exceed what the viewer may see.
- Search paginates by OFFSET, capped at 500 — not by the feed's `(created_at, id)` cursor, because results merge two tables and relevance order has no stable key. `nextCursor` is the next offset as a string; "Load more" appends.
- Business search returns real results (Phase 8B). Businesses are no longer a stub; `type=business` is exposed by `/api/search` and the filter panel.
- `/discover` is a Server Component that reads `?q=` and renders the `DiscoverExplorer` client island. The page passes `key={initialQuery}` so a top-bar submit while already on `/discover` re-runs the search instead of leaving stale results.
- `TopBarDesktop` stays a plain GET form to `/discover`. Never rebuild it as a JS-driven search box.
- Search requests carry a token so a late response cannot overwrite a newer one. Typing is debounced; Enter and clear flush immediately.
- `is_following` is computed per viewer in the same query as the results. A person card without it would always offer "Follow" for someone you already follow, and there would be no way to unfollow.
- Follow is optimistic and reverted on failure — same rule as likes.
- `/profile/[id]` is a FORWARDING route, not a profile UI. It redirects to
  `/user/[username]` when the account has a handle, and renders a minimal view
  only for accounts that have no username. An unknown account and a suspended
  account both 404.
- Run `npm run test:search` AND `npm run test:feed`. They share the visibility predicate; a change to one can break the other.
- Where `-webkit-line-clamp` is used, declare standard `line-clamp` alongside it.

## Design System (Phase 1)
- All colors come from CSS variables in `styles/tokens.css` or `styles/tokens-ui.css`. Never hardcode.
- Component primitives live in `src/components/ui/` with their own `.css` file using the `.ui-*` namespace.
- Zero runtime dependencies for primitives: use standard React and CSS.
- Keep components as Server Components by default; `"use client"` ONLY when state/interactivity is required.
- The `/design` route is dev-only and must not be linked from the main application.
- Brand color is FIXED (#6366F1). Users can only toggle light/dark theme.
- Gradients are reserved for brand/hero moments, never for standard buttons or form controls.
- Every component accepts a `className` prop.

## Repo layout (important)

- Git root is `C:\dev` (repo `noLIDA-platform/noLIDA.git`); the Next.js app lives in `nolida/`
- Never run `git init` inside `nolida/` — always commit and push from `C:\dev`
- Vercel **Root Directory** must be `nolida` (the repo root has no `package.json`)
- Local port 3000 is taken by Postgres — run dev with `npm run dev -- -p 3001`

## Brand (Phase 2)
- Brand colors are derived from the app icon (`public/branding/logo-source.jpg`). Never invent new colors.
- Brand assets are GENERATED, never hand-edited: run `npm run generate:brand` after replacing the source icon.
- Logo component lives at `src/components/brand/Logo/`. It renders no anchor — wrap it in a `Link` to make it navigable.
- Wordmark is "NOlida" (capital N and O, lowercase "lida"), set at `--weight-extrabold` with `-0.03em` tracking.
- The full gradient (cyan → indigo → violet → magenta) is reserved for the logo, hero backgrounds, step numerals, stat values, and special brand moments.
- Buttons and daily UI use SOLID `--color-primary`, never the gradient.
- The `inverse` and `outline-inverse` button variants are the only approved controls on navy/gradient surfaces.
- Dark mode background is `var(--color-navy)` — matches the icon.
- Gradient text (StatsRow) must keep a solid `--color-primary` fallback behind `@supports (background-clip: text)`.

## Marketing Site (Phase 2)
- Public pages live in `src/app/(marketing)/` and inherit its layout.
- Marketing layout wraps pages with `SiteHeader` + `SiteFooter`.
- Marketing pages never require authentication.
- CTA buttons link to `/signup` and `/login`. Both routes now exist: `/signup` renders, `/login` redirects to `/` (Phase 4B).
- Compose pages from `src/components/marketing/` section components; do not hand-roll page layouts.
- Shared page primitives live in `src/app/(marketing)/pages.css` (`.pg-*`). There is no landing page since Phase 4B, so `page.css` is gone.
- WhatsApp support link reads `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_WHATSAPP` via `buildWhatsAppHref()` in `src/lib/support/whatsapp.ts`. If not set, render a disabled button plus an explanation — never a broken link.
- `/terms` and `/privacy` are placeholders. Never write plausible-sounding legal copy.

## WhatsApp Support Links (Phase 7B)
- Every support WhatsApp CTA is a round, icon-only button using `WhatsAppButton` and `WhatsAppIcon` from `src/components/ui/WhatsAppButton/`.
- Read the support number from `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_WHATSAPP` via `buildWhatsAppHref()`; include the context-specific message from `docs/WHATSAPP-LINKS.md` as an encoded `text` query value.
- When the number is missing or invalid, render a disabled icon button with the tooltip `WhatsApp not configured`; never render a broken link.
- Keep support button labels accessible through `aria-label`; visible text may sit beside the icon where the page needs context, but never inside the button.
- User-content sharing to WhatsApp is distinct from support and may use its own share message.

## Headers (Phase 7C)
- Logged-in pages live under `src/app/(main)/` and use the `AppShell` from the `(main)` layout. They must NOT render their own header, logo, sidebar, or top bar.
- Public marketing pages live under `src/app/(marketing)/` and use `SiteHeader` + `SiteFooter` from the `(marketing)` layout.
- The desktop logo appears ONCE, in the `DesktopSidebar` top-left. `TopBarDesktop` does NOT render a logo.
- The mobile logo appears once, in `TopBarMobile` top-left.
- If a page has both a guest header and an app shell, that's a bug.

## Catalog (Phase 8A)
- Categories are a fixed seed list. Admins can add more in Phase 18.
- Services and products belong to a business and are editable by its owner at any status.
- Prices are stored in integer minor units (kobo for NGN); UI inputs accept major units and convert by multiplying by 100.
- Public visibility requires the business to be APPROVED (Phase 8B).
- Services have optional `price_min` and `price_max`; if only one is set, treat it as a fixed price. Products have one price.
- Product stock is optional; `null` means unlimited. Product image uploads are deferred; the editor does not upload images.

## Navigation Layout (Phase 7D)
- Desktop uses `DesktopSidebar` for primary navigation and `DesktopAvatarMenu` for account/activity links; it does not open the full-height mobile drawer.
- Mobile uses `TopBarMobile`, `MobileProfileDrawer`, and `MobileBottomNav`.
- Sign out lives only in the desktop avatar menu and mobile profile drawer, never in the sidebar.
- Profile is in the sidebar; do not add a duplicate View profile item to the desktop dropdown.
- Navigation items come from `src/lib/nav/items.ts`; do not hardcode nav items inside components. `MY_BUSINESS_TABS` (Phase 8C) is the dashboard's tab bar and lives there too.
- My Business appears only when the signed-in user owns an APPROVED business.

## Public Business Profile (Phase 8B)
- Public profile lives at /business/[slug].
- Only APPROVED businesses are reachable. Any other status → 404.
- Public pages do NOT use the app shell. They use the (public) layout
  with a minimal header (wordmark + login/signup or back-to-app link)
  and SiteFooter.
- Category listings live at /categories/[slug].
- Business search now returns real results (businesses are no longer
  a stub).
- /my-business/pending redirects automatically when status changes:
  - APPROVED → /my-business
  - DRAFT / CHANGES_REQUESTED → /my-business/submit
  - Otherwise shows status UI with a 15s poller.
- Reviews tab on the public profile is a placeholder until Phase 17.

## My Business Dashboard (Phase 8C)
- Dashboard lives at /my-business/* and requires an APPROVED business.
- Non-approved businesses are redirected:
  - DRAFT/CHANGES_REQUESTED → /my-business/submit
  - PENDING_REVIEW/REJECTED/SUSPENDED → /my-business/pending
- Dashboard navigation is a horizontal tab bar inside the main content
  area, NOT a second sidebar.
- Quick actions sit above the tabs.
- Overview pulls real data from services and products.
- Reviews, Bookings, Orders, Customers, Messages, Requests, Quotes,
  Earnings, Payouts, Analytics, Promotions are placeholders until
  their phases land.
- The Posts tab reuses the existing Feed component filtered by user_id.
- The dashboard layout lives at my-business/(dashboard)/layout.tsx. It MUST
  stay in that route group: at my-business/layout.tsx it would also wrap
  submit/ and pending/, whose own redirects target it — an infinite loop.
- APPROVED businesses are editable in place (BUSINESS_STATUS_EDITABLE),
  but still cannot be re-submitted for review (BUSINESS_STATUS_SUBMITTABLE).
  Editing a live profile must never drop it back to PENDING_REVIEW.

## Mobile Bottom Nav (Phase 8D)
- The bottom nav must fit every phone width from 320px up.
- 5 icons, equal spacing, no horizontal scroll.
- Respects safe-area-inset-bottom on notched devices.
- Any page that breaks the nav is fixed at the page level.
- The nav is `position: fixed` against the viewport, so ANY page wider than
  the viewport drags it out of alignment. Never use a negative
  `margin-inline` on a full-width child of `.app-shell__main`; that was the
  Phase 8D root cause (QuickActions, DiscoverySection).
- `html`/`body` carry `overflow-x: clip` as a backstop — `clip`, never
  `hidden`, because `hidden` on the root breaks `position: sticky`.

## My Business Tabs (Phase 8D)
- Analytics is the 2nd tab, right after Overview.
- Analytics shows real metrics even as a partial page. Never a full
  placeholder if data is available.
- Analytics is NOT flagged `placeholder` in MY_BUSINESS_TABS while its
  numbers are real. Charts arrive in Phase 19.

## Password and Sensitive Inputs (Phase 8D)
- Every password field uses <PasswordInput /> from
  '@/components/ui/PasswordInput/PasswordInput'.
- Never render <Input type="password" /> directly.
- PasswordInput Omit's `type` from its props so a caller cannot opt out of
  masking by accident.
- For future wallet balances, use <SensitiveValue /> from
  '@/components/ui/SensitiveValue/SensitiveValue' (built, intentionally unused
  until the wallet phase).
- Eye toggle icons: EyeIcon and EyeOffIcon from
  '@/components/ui/Icons/EyeIcon' and '@/components/ui/Icons/EyeOffIcon'.
  Inline SVG, not Lucide — `Icon` needs a LucideIcon, and these sit inside a
  labelled button.
- Toggle buttons have `type="button"` (a bare button inside a form submits it)
  and an action-named aria-label plus aria-pressed.

## Layout Overflow (Phase 8E)
- No page in NOlida causes horizontal scrolling on any mobile width.
- html and body BOTH use `overflow-x: clip` + `max-width`, never
  `hidden`. `hidden` makes the element a scroll container, so the
  document keeps a scrollport and can still be rubber-banded sideways —
  the page moves under your finger with no scrollbar drawn. `clip`
  removes the scrollport entirely and leaves `position: sticky` working.
- Any horizontal scroll container (tab bars, quick actions) uses its
  own `overflow-x: auto` + `max-width: 100vw` + `min-width: 0`, never
  pushing the page.
- The `min-width: 0` is the load-bearing half. A flex/grid item's automatic
  minimum size is its MIN-CONTENT width, so a wide child (17 tabs, a long
  unbroken URL) silently widens every ancestor up to the document. Adding
  `overflow-x: auto` alone does NOT fix this — the box grows instead of
  scrolling. Chain `min-width: 0` from the scroller up through every
  flex/grid ancestor.
- Grid and flex children that might blow out use `min-width: 0`, and grid
  tracks use `minmax(0, 1fr)` rather than a bare `1fr`.
- Never use a negative `margin-inline` for full-bleed; use padding.
- Never use JavaScript to detect or fix overflow — it is a CSS problem.
- Test every new page at 375px width before considering it done.

## Main Content Width (Phase 8E)
- On mobile (< 1024px) the main content fills 100% of the viewport.
  `.app-shell__main` is `width: 100%; max-width: 100vw;
  margin-inline: 0; align-self: stretch`.
- The 1080px reading column and its `margin-inline: auto` centring are
  scoped to `@media (min-width: 1080px)`. Putting them in the base rule
  made "fill the screen on a phone" an accident of arithmetic rather
  than a stated rule.
- On desktop the fixed DesktopSidebar is offset by PADDING on
  `.app-shell` inside `@media (min-width: 1024px)` — never a margin on
  the content, which would squeeze it by 260px on a phone.
- `.app-shell` and the dashboard wrappers declare `align-items/align-self:
  stretch` explicitly. A flex column whose items hug the left is exactly
  the "content in 40% of the screen" symptom.
- Content is centred by its OWN auto margins, never by
  `justify-content: center` on the row around it.

## Mobile Containment (Phase 8E)
- The page is never pannable; see the `overflow-x: clip` rule above.
- Deliberate horizontal scrollers (My Business tabs, quick actions,
  filter pills, discovery rows, public profile tabs) declare
  `touch-action: pan-x` so they keep their swipe. Everything else
  inherits the page-level policy.
- `body` uses `touch-action: manipulation`: removes double-tap-to-zoom
  and tap-highlight without disabling pinch zoom.
- Editable fields are NEVER under 16px. iOS Safari zooms the viewport
  on focus for any input below 16px and stays zoomed after blur, so the
  page looks broken and needs a double-tap to recover. This is a
  FONT-SIZE bug, not a viewport bug — fix it at `.ui-input` /
  `.ui-textarea`.
- The viewport export lives in `src/app/layout.tsx` and NOWHERE ELSE.
  Next.js reads `viewport` once, from the root; a `viewport` export in a
  nested `(main)`/`(auth)` layout is dead code that looks active.
- `maximumScale: 1` / `userScalable: false` ARE set (Phase 8G, product
  instruction) to stop pinch zoom. Most modern mobile browsers ignore both
  for accessibility reasons, so this is largely inert; where honoured it
  costs WCAG 1.4.4. Do not treat them as the fix for anything — the real
  auto-zoom bug was 14px inputs, fixed at `.ui-input`/`.ui-textarea`.

## Mobile Stability (Phase 8F)
- html, body have `overflow-x: clip` and `width: 100%`. Never `hidden`
  — it makes the element a scroll container the page can still be
  rubber-banded in.
- No page scrolls horizontally on any mobile width.
- Containers use `min-height: 100dvh`, NEVER `100vh`. `100vh` is the
  tallest viewport on iOS (measured with chrome hidden), so it over-runs
  and leaves dead space at the bottom.
- `.app-shell__main` is `flex: 1 0 auto`, not `flex: 1` / `1 1 0%`. A
  zero flex-basis stretched main to the full viewport BEFORE its padding
  was added, so short pages got guaranteed dead space under the fixed
  nav. Content determines height; the shell's `min-height` stretches.
- The bottom nav height plus `env(safe-area-inset-bottom)` is accounted
  for in main's `padding-bottom` — that is clearance for a fixed element,
  not layout height.
- Every horizontal scroller declares `overscroll-behavior-x: contain`.
  Without it, a swipe that reaches the end of the strip chains the
  remaining gesture to the document and the whole page slides sideways.
- Viewport meta uses `viewportFit: "cover"`.

## Perceived Speed (Phase 8F)
- Every route under `(main)` has a `loading.tsx` rendering `PageLoader`.
  A route uses its NEAREST loading boundary, so both the `(main)/` one
  and per-route ones exist and the closer one wins.
- `PageLoader` reuses the existing `Spinner` primitive. There is one
  spinner, not two.
- Nav Links use explicit `prefetch`.
- The AppShell renders immediately — it does no `await` at all. Do not
  add one. Notification/cart/message counts belong in a `<Suspense>`
  island or a client component that fetches after mount; awaiting them
  in `(main)/layout.tsx` delays the shell on EVERY navigation.
- Navigation should feel instant; only real network slowness should
  cause a visible wait.

## Business Request Flow (Phase 7E)
- /list-your-business shows a single in-app request form. No WhatsApp
  CTA, no manual code entry.
- On submit, the server generates a code, auto-redeems it for the
  user, creates a DRAFT business, and returns { code, businessId }.
- All of this happens in one Postgres transaction. The audit writes
  (code usage, security event) take the transaction client too — on
  the pool they would outlive a rollback.
- The code is created ALREADY SPENT: max_uses 1, uses_count 1,
  status USED. It is a receipt, not a credential.
  `authorizationCodes.repo.create` cannot set those columns, so they
  are written immediately after, inside the same transaction.
- The UI has a minimum 3-second loading state before revealing the
  code — even if the API responds instantly. `MIN_DELAY_MS` in
  BusinessRequestFlow.tsx; the CSS progress bar duplicates that
  duration and the two must change together.
- The code is displayed prominently with a copy button, and the user
  is told it is saved to their account.
- Continue navigates to /my-business/submit.
- `POST /api/businesses/request` is rate limited per user. It is the
  one endpoint where a signed-in user can mint a business, a code and
  an audit row per call.
- The old redeem-code endpoint and `RedeemCodeForm` stay for backward
  compatibility (admin-issued codes), even though no page currently
  renders the form. Do not delete them.
- `/list-your-business` is `force-dynamic` — it reads a session and
  redirects on status. Prerendered, it would serve every visitor the
  same answer and bounce signed-in users past their own business.

## OTP Bypass (Phase 7F, hardened in 7F.5)
- Controlled by two env vars: DEV_BYPASS_OTP and DEV_BYPASS_EMAILS.
- Bypass activates ONLY when:
  - DEV_BYPASS_OTP === "true"
  - The email being registered is an exact match in DEV_BYPASS_EMAILS
- DEV_BYPASS_EMAILS is comma-separated, case-insensitive, exact match.
  Entries are trimmed and lowercased; the identifier is normalised the
  same way, so casing and stray spaces never block a legitimate dev.
- Empty DEV_BYPASS_EMAILS disables bypass entirely. Missing var does too.
- There is deliberately NO wildcard or partial matching: `*`, a bare
  domain like `gmail.com`, and a prefix like `nolida.dev` must all fail.
  Exact match only — a loose rule here is an auth bypass.
- Safe to use in any environment (localhost, preview, production)
  because only listed emails can bypass and everyone else takes the
  normal OTP path.
- BOTH safeguards are load-bearing and independent:
  - The whitelist decides WHO can bypass.
  - `isProductionDeployment()` decides WHERE, refusing to run when
    VERCEL_ENV=production or NODE_ENV=production. Neither is settable as
    an environment variable on Vercel, so copying a .env.local into
    production still evaluates false.
  Do not remove either on the argument that the other is sufficient.
- Only EMAIL registrations can bypass. A phone signup always takes the
  normal OTP flow, because a phone number never matches an email list.
- Logs a loud console.warn naming the identifier that bypassed, and
  writes `metadata: { devBypass: true }` on the USER_REGISTERED security
  event so affected accounts are findable in the audit trail.
- The signup page then calls /api/auth/login and goes to /home. It obeys
  `verified` from the server response; the client never decides this.
- When inactive, the normal OTP flow runs unchanged. No OTP code was
  removed.
- LoginForm shows "Account created. Please log in." for ?registered=1 —
  the fallback when the account was made but the auto sign-in failed.
- `bypassEnabled` and `bypassEmails` are module-level constants read at
  import time, so changing either env var requires a dev server restart.
- Never put a real personal email in a committed .env.example.

## Media Uploads (Phase 5C)
- All file uploads go through /api/upload (nodejs runtime, maxDuration 60).
- Storage is Cloudinary. Provider is abstracted in
  src/lib/server/adapters/storage/. Only cloudinary.adapter.ts may
  import the cloudinary SDK, and only index.ts chooses the provider.
- Uploads are SIGNED through our own API. Never add an unsigned upload
  preset and never install next-cloudinary — both hand upload rights to
  anyone with a browser and no session.
- Only URLs are stored in the database. Never write a file to the server
  filesystem.
- Validation rules (file.validator.ts, server-side, always):
  - Images: jpeg, png, webp, gif. Max 5MB.
  - Videos: mp4, webm, mov. Max 50MB.
  - Reject any filename containing .. / \\ or NUL.
  - `sniffMimeType` reads magic bytes and MUST agree with both the
    declared MIME and the extension. `File.type` is attacker-controlled.
  - SVG is never allowed — it is inline script.
- The client list in src/lib/client/upload.ts is a COURTESY copy and is
  re-checked on the server. A Client Component may not import from
  src/lib/server/, which is why the copy exists. Keep the two in step.
- Every stored media URL must be https-only. `posts.media`,
  `products.images`, `avatar_url` and `businesses.photos` all render into
  an `src`, so a `javascript:` value is a stored XSS. Enforce it in the
  service, not only in the zod schema.
- Client-side uploads use <ImageUploader />. Never build a custom
  uploader inline. It uses XMLHttpRequest because fetch cannot report
  upload progress; the bar is real bytes capped at 90%.
- Post media: up to 4 items (images + videos mixed). A post containing any
  video drops the grid — a 9:16 clip in a grid cell is a postage stamp.
- Product images: up to 5 items.
- Business photos: 1 cover, 1 logo, up to 8 gallery, stored as
  { cover, logo, gallery }. `businesses.photos` used to default to '[]',
  so readers must tolerate the legacy bare array.
- Replacing an avatar deletes the old asset via `publicIdFromUrl`, which
  returns null for anything that is NOT one of our Cloudinary URLs — never
  delete a file we do not own.
- Cloudinary env vars must be set in .env.local and Vercel for uploads to
  work. If missing, /api/upload returns 500 STORAGE_NOT_CONFIGURED.
- An upload returns BOTH `url` and `secureUrl`. ALWAYS store `secureUrl`.
  Cloudinary's plain `url` is `http://` on many accounts while every media
  schema here is https-only, so reading the wrong field turns a successful
  upload into a validation error at Publish — that was the Phase 5C.1 bug,
  reported as "Media must be an https URL". `uploadMediaFile` now normalises
  `url` to https too, so both fields are safe, but `secureUrl` is the contract.
- See docs/MEDIA.md.

## Media Uploads (Phase 5C.2 update)
- Uploads go DIRECTLY from the client to Cloudinary.
- The server only issues signatures via /api/upload/signature.
- Use uploadDirect() from src/lib/client/upload.ts — never POST file
  bytes to our own server for uploads.
- /api/upload still exists as a fallback but should not be used.
- Always pass result.secureUrl (never result.url) to onChange.
- Real progress via XMLHttpRequest's upload.onprogress.
- Multiple files upload in parallel via Promise.all.
- THE SIGNATURE IS THE CONTRACT: Cloudinary recomputes it from the
  fields it RECEIVES, so every field in `params` must be echoed back
  verbatim. A signed-but-unsent field is "Invalid Signature", not a
  warning. The client iterates `signature.params` rather than listing
  field names, so the two sides cannot drift.
- `allowed_formats` is signed, which makes Cloudinary enforce it. That
  is what replaces `sniffMimeType` on the direct path — do not remove
  it, and do not add a format to one allow-list without the other.
- There is NO server-enforced byte ceiling on the direct path.
  Cloudinary has no per-request size parameter (`max_file_size` is not
  real — not in the SDK's types, JS, or docs). Size is a client-side
  courtesy check plus the account's plan limit. See docs/MEDIA.md.
- The folder and the format allow-list are derived from `purpose` on
  the server. Never accept a folder from the request body.
- Only `purpose=post` may request `resourceType=video`.
- Every signature writes UPLOAD_SIGNATURE_ISSUED to security_events.
  A failed audit write is logged, never allowed to deny an upload.

## Media Viewer (Phase 5C.3)
- The MediaViewer uses a horizontal track with `translate3d` for
  navigation. Every item is in the DOM; only one is on screen.
- THE PERCENTAGE IS THE TRICK: `-100%` resolves against the TRACK's
  width, and each slide is `flex: 0 0 100%` of that same width, so
  `-index * 100` lands exactly on one slide. Never switch a slide to a
  fixed pixel width — the two must stay tied.
- Rendering only the current item makes a slide impossible. There is
  nothing to move, so the browser cuts instead.
- Navigation does NOT wrap. The arrows disable at each end
  (`disabled`, not inert) and a swipe at an end does nothing. A wrapped
  carousel needs an infinite track, and teleporting from item 4 to item
  1 is wrong for a screen reader.
- Slide transition: 300ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1).
- Swipe threshold: 50px.
- Fade-in on open (200ms), fade-out on close (180ms).
- The close timer lives in the component; the fade is CSS. They must
  stay in step: `CLOSE_ANIMATION_MS` and the keyframe duration.
- Under reduced motion, the component skips the wait entirely
  (`matchMedia`) as well as the CSS disabling the animation.
- Body scroll is locked while open, and restored on close.
- Respects prefers-reduced-motion — no animation when enabled.
- Keyboard: ArrowLeft, ArrowRight, Escape. Escape routes through
  `close()`, never straight to `onClose`, or the fade is cut at frame 0.
- Focus is captured on open, TRAPPED inside the overlay, and restored
  to the triggering thumbnail on unmount. The dialog alone does not
  confine Tab; the handler cycles the close button and two arrows.
- Only the visible slide is mounted as a player: `autoPlay` and
  `controls` are withheld off-screen, and off-screen slides are
  `aria-hidden`. Four clips playing behind each other is a wall of
  noise.
- Arrows stay visible on mobile. Hiding them below 768px removes the
  only visible affordance; swipe is not discoverable. They move to the
  bottom at 520px instead.
- `content-visibility: auto` on a slide is load-bearing: without it, a
  four-item track paints four fullscreen images for one being shown.

## Profile Page (Phase 5D)
- `/profile` is the current user's own profile.
- `/user/[username]` is any user's public profile, keyed by HANDLE,
  not by id. A profile URL is the one thing people put in a message,
  and a UUID cannot be said out loud.
- `/profile/[id]` FORWARDS to `/user/[username]`. Do not build a second
  profile UI there; two UIs for one person is how /profile/[id] fell
  behind /profile in the first place.
- Your own handle redirects to `/profile`, so exactly one page ever
  offers "Edit profile".
- Layout: header, tabs, then ONE panel for the active tab.
- The active tab is `?tab=posts|saved|tagged|about`. Not client state:
  a tab bar is navigation, and navigation that needs JavaScript to
  have happened is navigation that has not happened yet. It must
  survive a refresh and be shareable.
- ProfileTabs is a Server Component of plain `<Link>`s. Do not add
  "use client" to it.
- Only the active tab's list is fetched. Fetching every tab's data
  pays for lists nobody is looking at.
- "Saved" appears only on your own profile. It is always empty on
  someone else's and invites a question we cannot answer.
- Posts tab is a 3-column grid of square tiles, three columns at
  EVERY width. A 2-column phone grid makes each tile look like a feed
  post; the density is the point.
- Text-only posts render their own body as the tile. Videos get a play
  badge.
- `postsCount` MUST come from `posts.repo.countByUser({ userId,
  viewerId })`, which applies `VISIBLE_TO_VIEWER`. A count that
  ignored visibility would contradict the grid beneath it and would
  leak the existence of private posts.
- Never filter visibility in the page. `getUserPosts` already applies
  the predicate in SQL; filtering after loading is how a private post
  leaks through a second door.
- Only an APPROVED business appears in the header. A DRAFT or
  PENDING_REVIEW business is the owner's own unfinished page and must
  not be advertised.
- Follow is optimistic and reverts on failure, like like and save.
  The hover "Unfollow" label is a second `aria-hidden` span, not a JS
  text swap — the button keeps ONE accessible name describing its
  current state.
- A tooltip on a DISABLED button never shows; put the `title` on a
  wrapper span.
- Profile view types live in `@/lib/profile/types`, NOT in
  `profile.service.ts` — no component may import from `src/lib/server/`.
- Verification (email/phone) shows on your OWN About tab only. It
  used to live in a "My Account" card on /profile, which this phase
  removed. Do not drop it.

## Requests and Responses (Phase 9)
- Requests are their own entity (not a post type), but creating a
  request also creates a REQUEST_POST in the feed for discovery.
  Both rows go in ONE transaction — neither half is useful alone.
- Statuses: OPEN, IN_PROGRESS, FULFILLED, CLOSED, CANCELLED.
  OPEN and IN_PROGRESS both accept new offers.
- Urgencies: URGENT, NORMAL, FLEXIBLE.
- Response statuses: PENDING, ACCEPTED, DECLINED, WITHDRAWN.
- ONLY a user with an APPROVED business can respond, and the business
  is resolved from the SESSION. There is no `businessId` in the
  response body and there must never be — it would let any signed-in
  user answer as any business in the database.
- One response per business per request (unique constraint). The
  service checks `hasResponded` for a readable message and CATCHES the
  unique violation for the race between two simultaneous taps.
- The request owner cannot respond to their own request.
- Accepting a response sets `accepted_response_id`, moves the request
  to IN_PROGRESS, and sets every OTHER response to DECLINED — in one
  transaction. `declineOthers` must include 'ACCEPTED' in its WHERE
  clause, or accepting a second offer leaves TWO accepted responses.
- ONLY THE OWNER SEES THE RESPONSES. Everyone else gets the count plus
  their own offer. A competing business reading rivals' prices is the
  one thing this marketplace must never expose — and the filter is in
  the service, never in the page.
- `requests.response_count` is denormalised. Written inside the same
  transaction as the response that changes it, and clamped with
  GREATEST(0, …).
- Responses list OLDEST FIRST (the shared cursor is reused; only the
  comparison flips to `>`). The first responder asked first and has
  waited longest.
- Money in requests is MAJOR units (naira), unlike products and
  services which are minor units. `formatMoney` in
  `@/lib/requests/constants` documents why, and must not "fix" it.
- `deadline` is cast to text in SQL. node-postgres parses DATE into a
  JS Date at LOCAL midnight, which shifts a day backwards west of UTC
  — "due 15 March" would render as 14 March in Lagos.
- No conversation is created on acceptance. Messaging is Phase 10 and
  auto-opening a thread nobody asked for is that phase's decision.
- Request view types live in `@/lib/requests/types` — no component may
  import from `src/lib/server/`. Bounds live in
  `@/lib/requests/constants` so the composer's counters cannot drift
  from what the server accepts.
- `purpose="request"` is a real upload folder (`nolida/requests`) and
  is images-only. Adding it to `UPLOAD_FOLDERS` requires adding it to
  the signature endpoint's allow-list too, or uploads fail.

## UI Reference System (Phase 5G)
- All user-facing features have a designated reference in
  docs/UI-PATTERNS.md.
- Before building or polishing any user-facing feature, check the
  reference.
- Follow the reference's UX patterns — adapt to NOlida's brand.
- NEVER copy code, assets, logos, exact colors, or fonts.
- Nigerian context wins (WhatsApp voice notes, naira-first amounts).
- When in doubt, choose the simpler pattern.
- Every new feature must be added to docs/UI-PATTERNS.md before
  implementation.
- A reference is a guide for LAYOUT and INTERACTION, not a licence
  to restructure a working page. Polish means visual only: a pass that
  changes a form payload, an API call or a state transition is a
  rebuild, and a rebuild belongs in its own phase.
- Reference tokens are never copied literally. Use the tokens this
  app already defines; a one-off hex from a reference app is a new
  token wearing a disguise.
 - Where a reference conflicts with a rule in this file (a negative
  margin for full-bleed media, for example), the rule in this file
  wins. The reference describes how it looks, not how this codebase
  is built.

## Messaging (Phase 10)
- Conversations are DIRECT (1:1) for now. Group chats are future.
- Message kinds: TEXT, VOICE, IMAGE, FILE, SHARED_ITEM, SYSTEM.
- Voice notes store { url, duration, waveform } as JSONB.
- Attachments store an array of { url, type, size, name }.
- Shared items store { type, id, title, subtitle, imageUrl }.
- Reactions: ❤️ 😂 😮 😢 👍 🙏 (fixed set for v1).
- Reply: reply_to_message_id points to the original message.
- Forward: is_forwarded + forwarded_from_message_id.
- Edit window: 15 minutes. Delete is a soft delete.
- Read receipts: message_receipts tracks delivered_at and read_at.
- Typing indicators use the typing_status table. Entries older than
  5 seconds are considered stale.
- Real-time: Supabase Realtime if env vars are set. Fallback to 5s
  polling otherwise.
- Voice notes use MediaRecorder + getUserMedia. Uploaded to
  Cloudinary via the existing /api/upload/signature flow.
- Block/report actions inside the chat menu write to the reports
  table (Phase 17 will surface them in the admin panel).
- Calls are NOT in this phase. Deferred to Phase 10.5.
- UI reference: WhatsApp (primary), iMessage (secondary).

